  "id": "COMPOUND:42934 - PROTEIN:6340",
  "verbalization": "Cordiachrome C is implicated in the inhibition of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex through a multi-step mechanism involving DNA polymerase iota. The evidence suggests that Cordiachrome C acts as a target for multiple compounds, including N-[(4R,10R,13S)-17-[1-(dimethylamino)ethyl]-4-hydroxy-10,13-dimethyl-2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9,11,12,14,15,16,17-tetradecahydro-1H-cyclopenta[a]phenanthren-3-yl]-N,3-dimethylbut-2-enamide and methyl (1S,5R,6S,7S,8R,11S,12R,14S)-11,12-diacetyloxy-4,7-dihydroxy-6-[(6S,8R,9R)-2-hydroxy-11-methyl-5,7,10-trioxatetracyclo[6.3.1.02,6.09,11]dodec-3-en-9-yl]-6-methyl-14-[(E)-2-methylbut-2-enoyl]oxy-3,9-dioxatetracyclo[6.6.1.01,5.011,15]pentadecane-4-carboxylate, both of which are known to target DNA polymerase iota. These compounds are further linked to the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, indicating a potential pathway where Cordiachrome C may modulate the activity of this complex through the inhibition of DNA polymerase iota. The evidence suggests that Cordiachrome C may exert its effects by interfering with the function of DNA polymerase iota, which in turn may influence the activity of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Cordiachrome C in modulating chromatin structure and gene expression through the inhibition of DNA polymerase iota and its downstream targets.",
